  overview: "Proximal humerus fractures (PHF) account for about 4% to 5% of all fractures, and about 40% of them are displaced fractures involving the surgical neck [r1]. Most result from falls from standing height, and the age-specific incidence is increasing as with other fragility fractures [r2]. The trials mostly enrolled women aged 60 or over [r3]. Fractures are classified by Neer into one-part (minimally displaced) fractures and two-, three- and four-part fractures according to the number of displaced segments (head, greater tuberosity, lesser tuberosity and shaft) [r4]. In the Cochrane trials of surgery, 66% of the displaced fractures were three- or four-part [r3].\n\nThe best evidence is the pragmatic PROFHER trial. It randomised 250 adults (mean age 66 years, 77% women) with a displaced fracture involving the surgical neck to surgery (fixation or humeral head replacement) or a sling, with standardised rehabilitation for both. The Oxford Shoulder Score averaged over 2 years was 39.07 after surgery and 38.32 without (difference 0.75, 95% CI −1.33 to 2.84, against a minimal clinically important difference of 5 points); complications, secondary surgery and mortality did not differ significantly [r5]. At 5 years there were still no statistically or clinically significant differences [r6]. Earlier surgery did not improve outcomes: mean time to surgery was 10.5 days, and timing did not affect the Oxford Shoulder Score at any point irrespective of age or fracture type [r7]. The Cochrane review pooled ten trials (717 participants) and found high-certainty evidence of no clinically important difference in function at 1 and 2 years, and low-certainty evidence of more additional surgery with surgery (risk ratio 2.06) [r3]. The limits matter: surgeons excluded 17% of otherwise eligible patients for lack of equipoise, and 56% of eligible patients did not consent, mostly older people who preferred non-surgical care [r8]. The Cochrane review found evidence absent or insufficient for people under 60 years, high-energy trauma, two-part tuberosity fractures, fracture-dislocations and articular surface fractures [r3].\n\nEvidence for reverse shoulder arthroplasty (RSA) in older adults with three- or four-part fractures is mixed. A long-term follow-up of an earlier randomised trial in people aged 80 or over (12 managed conservatively and 17 with RSA available) found a better Constant score with RSA (62 versus 51) at a mean of about 7.5 years, with all non-operatively treated fractures healing in malposition and none requiring revision [r9]. A 2026 randomised trial of 80 people aged 60 to 90 found better 1-year function with RSA (WOOS 76.9 versus 56.2), with 5 crossovers from the non-operative group [r10]. A meta-analysis of six mostly retrospective comparisons reported better forward flexion with RSA (121.5° versus 100°) and no difference in overall complications [r11]. The PROFHER-2 trial of RSA versus hemiarthroplasty versus non-surgical care in people over 65 has a published protocol [r2], but no results were found in PubMed.\n\nFor the physiotherapist, rehabilitation is central whichever pathway is chosen. For non-operatively treated fractures, a meta-analysis of six randomised trials (470 patients) found that mobilisation within a week of injury was safe and gave a short-term functional benefit at 3 months (standardised mean difference 0.4) compared with starting after 3 to 4 weeks [r12], although the Cochrane review rated the evidence for early versus delayed mobilisation as very low certainty [r3]. Two small trials found little difference between instructed home exercises and supervised physiotherapy [r13]. After fixation of valgus-impacted fractures, a retrospective cohort reported better 24-month Constant scores with active exercise from day 1 (88.7 versus 75.3), but this is a low-level study [r14]. Complications include avascular necrosis of the humeral head, found in 6.6% of 121 people after plate fixation of three- and four-part fractures and associated with four-part fractures, medial hinge disruption and tuberosity comminution [r15], and in 11% of 44 people in another series, where Neer four-part fractures had an odds ratio of 8 [r16].",

