    text: "The proximal humerus comprises the articular head, the greater and lesser tuberosities, the surgical neck and the upper shaft. The tuberosities carry the rotator cuff insertions (supraspinatus, infraspinatus and teres minor on the greater tuberosity; subscapularis on the lesser tuberosity), and the long head of biceps lies in the groove between them. The head is supplied by the anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries, so fractures that disrupt the medial hinge and the head's blood supply carry a higher risk of avascular necrosis [r15,r16]. The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck and supplies deltoid and the skin over the lateral shoulder, and the axillary artery lies close to the medial side. The proximal humeral physis (growth plate) is a separate issue in children.",
