  prognosis: "Most displaced fractures in older adults recover well without surgery. In PROFHER, the average Oxford Shoulder Score over 2 years was 38.32 without surgery, 39.07 with it (difference 0.75, 95% CI −1.33 to 2.84) [r5], and there were no significant differences at 3, 4 or 5 years [r6]. In operated participants, most had their maximum functional outcome at 6 months and maintained it at 5 years [r7]. Fractures managed non-operatively often heal in malposition: all 12 non-operatively treated fractures healed in malposition at a mean of 7.4 years in people aged 80 or over, with none needing revision to RSA, although function was lower with the Constant score 51 versus 62 after RSA [r9]. Surgery is associated with more additional operations (risk ratio 2.06) [r3]. Avascular necrosis occurred in 6.6% and 11% in two surgical series, more often in four-part fractures [r15,r16], and it can appear late. Rarely, a malunited fracture can lead to biceps tendon rupture [r17]. The findings come mainly from older adults with displaced fractures, and outcomes in people under 60 or after high-energy trauma are less certain [r3].",
  prevention: [
    "Reduce falls risk in older adults: balance and strength training, vision and medicine review, and home hazard assessment (reasonable principle; trial evidence for fracture reduction is not covered in the sources reviewed)",
    "Assess bone health after any fragility fracture and treat osteoporosis (reasonable principle) [r2]",
    "Support smoking cessation, adequate nutrition including calcium and vitamin D, and regular weight-bearing activity (reasonable principle)",
    "Review medicines that increase falls (sedatives, antihypertensives) (clinical convention)",
    "For younger people, use protective equipment and safe practices in cycling, snow sports and horse riding (reasonable principle)"
  ],
  clinicalPearls: [
    "Most displaced proximal humerus fractures in older adults do not need surgery: the Cochrane review found high-certainty evidence of no clinically important functional difference at 1 and 2 years, and low-certainty evidence of more additional operations with surgery [r3].",
    "PROFHER is the benchmark trial: no difference in Oxford Shoulder Score at 2 years (39.07 versus 38.32, minimal clinically important difference 5) [r5] or at 5 years [r6], and earlier surgery did not help [r7].",
    "Know the limits of the evidence: under 60 years, high-energy trauma, two-part tuberosity fractures and fracture-dislocations are not covered by the trials [r3], and over half of eligible patients declined randomisation, mainly older patients preferring non-surgical care [r8].",
    "The RSA story is moving: a 2026 randomised trial found better 1-year function with RSA (WOOS 76.9 versus 56.2) [r10], while long-term follow-up of an earlier trial in people aged 80 or over showed a Constant score of 62 versus 51 but included only 29 people [r9]; PROFHER-2 is the trial to watch [r2].",
    "Early mobilisation is reasonable for stable fractures: a meta-analysis found it safe with a short-term benefit at 3 months [r12], but the Cochrane review rated the evidence very low certainty [r3]. Confirm stability first.",
    "Watch for avascular necrosis and plan for stiffness: AVN was 6.6% to 11% after plate fixation and half of cases appeared within the first 15 months, so symptoms can return later [r15,r16]. Always test the axillary nerve once safe."
  ],
